body_text:
## Issue description

`get_session_list()` can create a self-sustaining plugin event loop when the caller subscribes to `SessionUpdate` and queries the session list while updating or rendering:

```text
render -> get_session_list -> UpdateSessionInfos -> SessionUpdate -> render
```

The API returns the requested `SessionListSnapshot` synchronously, but it also sends `ScreenInstruction::UpdateSessionInfos`. `Screen::update_session_infos` currently broadcasts `Event::SessionUpdate` on every refresh, including when the incoming session data is identical to its cache.

This is adjacent to #5278/#5279 but concerns the event emitted after a successful query rather than whether the returned snapshot contains the current session.

Tested on Linux against current main `af38660c5884f50bb3726682fb92961326c4268f`. The probe uses `zellij-tile` 0.44.3 because that is the first patch series containing this API; the same WASM reproduces on the current-main host.

## Minimal reproduction

This capped plugin uses one timer to seed one render. Every later render is caused by the `SessionUpdate` returned from the preceding query:

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use zellij_tile::prelude::*;

#[derive(Default)]
struct State {
    started: bool,
    updates: usize,
}

register_plugin!(State);

impl ZellijPlugin for State {
    fn load(&mut self, _: BTreeMap<String, String>) {
        subscribe(&[
            EventType::PermissionRequestResult,
            EventType::SessionUpdate,
            EventType::Timer,
        ]);
        request_permission(&[PermissionType::ReadApplicationState]);
        set_timeout(0.5);
    }

    fn update(&mut self, event: Event) -> bool {
        match event {
            Event::Timer(_) if !self.started => {
                self.started = true;
                true
            }
            Event::SessionUpdate(_, _) if self.started => {
                self.updates += 1;
                self.updates < 200 // safety cap
            }
            _ => false,
        }
    }

    fn render(&mut self, _: usize, _: usize) {
        if self.started {
            let result = get_session_list();
            println!("updates={} query={:?}", self.updates, result.is_ok());
        }
    }
}
```

Build it with `zellij-tile = "=0.44.3"`, load it in a pane, grant `ReadApplicationState`, and restart the disposable session once so the permission is already cached before the seed timer fires. The update counter reaches the cap without further input.

The instrumented version used for the measurements also records each event and query to its private plugin data directory. It:

1. subscribes to `Timer` and `SessionUpdate`;
2. uses one timer to request exactly one render;
3. calls `get_session_list()` once in each render;
4. returns `true` from `SessionUpdate` handling;
5. stops requesting renders after 200 updates as a safety cap.

On current main `af38660c`, a 1.5-second sample produced:

```text
successful get_session_list calls: 179
SessionUpdate events:              178
query errors:                        0
```

There is no repeating timer or other render source after the first seed. The last log records alternate exactly:

```text
render-before-query ...
query-ok ...
session-update ...
render-before-query ...
query-ok ...
session-update ...
```

## Expected behavior

Refreshing an unchanged session-list snapshot should not produce another `SessionUpdate`. PR #5063 introduced `get_session_list()` to replace passive polling and describes `SessionUpdate` as being sent only on actual changes.

## Proposed fix

Before broadcasting from `Screen::update_session_infos`, compare the incoming live-session metadata with the cached metadata and compare the set of resurrectable-session names. Always store the fresh caches, but do not treat an elapsed resurrection duration alone as a session-list change because that duration advances on every scan.

With that patch, the same real-WASM probe produces:

```text
successful get_session_list calls: 2
SessionUpdate events:              1
query errors:                      0
```

The single event is the first changed snapshot. Its follow-up query is identical, so the chain ends. A focused regression also verifies that a real `SessionInfo` change still emits another event.

## Related visible effect

