body_text:
I went through a diff of my config to attempt to incorporate the new `45` OSC prompt features. It was a bit challenging due to the differences in how, presumably, [`mod.rs`](https://github.com/zellij-org/zellij/blob/34e75e991f270acdcfa22b5e6f665007235a55ed/zellij-utils/src/kdl/mod.rs#L4518-L4524) generates the a config vs the [`default.kdl`](https://github.com/zellij-org/zellij/blob/34e75e991f270acdcfa22b5e6f665007235a55ed/zellij-utils/assets/config/default.kdl) coming from `setup --dump-config`. Not sure if there is a way to sync these up or choose to generate or the other.

One issue is the below are missing from `default.kdl`.
```kdl
// How often in seconds sessions are serialized
// 
// serialization_interval 10000
// A fixed name to always give the Zellij session.
// Consider also setting `attach_to_session true,`
// otherwise this will error if such a session exists.
// Default: <RANDOM>
// 
// session_name "My singleton session"

// When `session_name` is provided, attaches to that session
// if it is already running or creates it otherwise.
// Default: false
//
// attach_to_session true
// Whether to show tips on startup
// Default: true
// 
// show_startup_tips false

// Whether to show visual bell indicators (pane/tab frame flash and [!] suffix)
// default is true
// visual_bell true

// Whether to focus panes on mouse hover
// default is false
// focus_follows_mouse false

// Whether clicking a pane to focus it also sends the click into the pane
// default is false
// mouse_click_through false
```

There are also other differences in the order of the sections which I got around by syncing up to the `--dump-config` output. `tmux` section was missing as well.

Additionally, config like the below doesn't allow you to type into the Session Manager. Had to change it to `Locked`.
https://github.com/zellij-org/zellij/blob/34e75e991f270acdcfa22b5e6f665007235a55ed/zellij-utils/assets/config/default.kdl#L135

Lastly, there are many differences in the `shared_except` and `shared_among` config that is a bit confusing.

Not sure the strategy for the user here. Maybe I am missing something obvious or this is indeed something the user should own more and standardize on the `--dump-config` output. My goal was to make sure I had all the available config and if new config came out it was easy to diff my current config to see any new options though there is the documentation and change log too.

